<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yashwant Rao Holkar II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yashwant Rao Holkar II was the last ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Indore, from 1926. He acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947, and when Indore merged into Madhya Bharat in 1948 he served as its Uprajpramukh, deputy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jivajirao-scindia">Jivajirao Scindia</a> of Gwalior, until the union joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar-ii.jpg" alt="Yashwant Rao Holkar II" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DEVARE &amp; CO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Investiture_of_his_Highness_Maharaja_Yeshwant_Rao_Holkar_Bahadur_of_Indore_9th_May_1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yashwant Rao Holkar II was born on 6 September 1908 into the Holkar dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and was educated at Cheam School, Charterhouse and Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. In 1924 he married Sanyogita Bai Sahib Holkar of Indore, who also studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The couple travelled widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and became known for a life of elegance and extravagance in the 1920s and 1930s; Man Ray photographed them in 1927, and Bernard Boutet de Monvel painted two sets of double portraits of them in 1929 and 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His father, Tukojirao Holkar III, abdicated in his favour on 26 February 1926, and he was installed on 11 March 1926 under a regency council, receiving full powers on 9 May 1930. He set up a legislative council for Indore State and a cabinet of a prime minister and three ministers, and in 1935 he was made a Knight Grand Commander of the Order of the Indian Empire. The British resident K.S. Fitze noted how much time he spent abroad. On 11 August 1947 he signed the Instrument of Accession, and on 28 May 1948 Indore State was included in the Union of Madhya Bharat.</p><p>In 1930 he commissioned the Manik Bagh palace in Indore from the German architect Eckart Muthesius, who was only a few years older than the young maharaja. Inside and out it was built in the late Art Deco and international style; in 2019 the Musée des Arts Décoratifs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> devoted the exhibition Moderne Maharajah to him and the palace, showing pieces such as an aluminium bed by Louis Sognot and Charlotte Alix.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sanyogita died in 1937 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> after an appendectomy. In 1938 he married Marguerite Lawler of Fargo, North Dakota, and after their divorce Euphemia Watt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> in 1943. He later worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, though the details of his role are unclear. He died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> hospital on 5 December 1961. He was survived by his daughter Usha Raje, who inherited his titles, and by Shivaji Rao Holkar, his son by his third wife. Manik Bagh later became an office of the Indian customs and excise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yashwant Rao Holkar II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yashwant Rao Holkar II</b> acceded to India, and in 1948 Indore merged into Madhya Bharat, joining Madhya Pradesh in 1956.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li><li><blockquote>Jivajirao Scindia of Gwalior served as Rajpramukh and <b>Yashwant Rao Holkar II</b> as Uprajpramukh.</blockquote><a href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Indore, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Indore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6579 1325 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yashwant Rao Holkar II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7029 1595h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7029 1595h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.72,75.86&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
